{"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"BackoffJitter","kind":"identifier"}],"platforms":["Linux"]}]},{"content":[{"type":"heading","anchor":"overview","text":"Overview","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Jitter takes a nominal wait and picks the wait to use instead. It works on any clock’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"Duration","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and the operators that wait take it as a ","type":"text"},{"code":"jitter:","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" parameter:"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Afluent\/documentation\/Afluent\/ExponentialBackoffStrategy","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" (each backoff delay), "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Afluent\/documentation\/Afluent\/AsyncSequences\/TimerSequence","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" (where"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each tick falls inside its period) and "},{"code":"delay","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" on units of work and sequences (each wait)."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The kinds follow “Exponential Backoff and Jitter” (AWS Architecture Blog):","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Afluent\/documentation\/Afluent\/BackoffJitter\/none","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":": the nominal wait, exactly."}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Afluent\/documentation\/Afluent\/BackoffJitter\/full(using:)","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":": anywhere from zero up to the nominal wait."}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Afluent\/documentation\/Afluent\/BackoffJitter\/equal(using:)","isActi